# PROGRESS.md was frozen on 2026-08-23. The live progress log is now one file per
# session at docs/sessions/CC-<YYYYMMDD>-<id>.md, and PROGRESS.md is a sealed archive
# that nothing appends to. Sessions no longer share a write target, so the conflict
# class this attribute existed to soften no longer arises for new work.
#
# WHY IT IS STILL HERE: branches cut before the cutover still carry PROGRESS.md edits.
# Until those drain, the union driver keeps their local merges resolving without
# hand-editing. Once no open PR touches PROGRESS.md, this line can be deleted.
#
# WHAT IT NEVER BOUGHT, and the reason for the migration. Git honours merge drivers
# LOCALLY ONLY. GitHub does not run them server-side: it computes mergeability with a
# plain three-way merge and reports CONFLICTING for any PR whose PROGRESS.md region
# also moved on main. A PR could therefore merge perfectly cleanly in a worktree and
# still be blocked in the UI. Clearing that required merging main into the branch and
# pushing — and branch protection sets dismiss_stale_reviews AND
# require_last_push_approval, so that push permanently destroyed any approval the PR
# carried. Force-pushing back did not restore it. That cost four approvals in one week
# and once stranded thirteen at a time.
#
# An early version of this comment claimed the attribute "eliminates the recurring
# PROGRESS.md merge conflicts across concurrent Claude sessions". It never did. It hid
# them locally, which was worse than not having it, because the branch looked healthy
# right up until GitHub refused to merge it.
#
# See MERGE_WORKFLOW.md, "PROGRESS.md conflicts (RESOLVED 2026-08-23)".
PROGRESS.md merge=union

# Per-session logs deliberately get NO merge driver. One session writes one file, so
# there is nothing to union-merge; a conflict here would mean two sessions picked the
# same Session ID, which should surface loudly rather than be silently concatenated.

# Git hooks are executed by /bin/sh. With core.autocrlf=true (the default on
# Windows here) a checkout would rewrite these to CRLF, and a CRLF shebang
# fails with "bad interpreter". Pin them to LF so the hook survives a clone.
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the Claude Code hooks: .claude/settings.json invokes them via
# `bash <path>`, and stray CR bytes in the body surface as `$'\r': command not found`.
.claude/hooks/** text eol=lf
